==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbɝɡəˌmɑt/|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbɜːɡəˌmɒt/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-bergamot.ogg|a=US}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{bor|en|fr|bergamote}}, from {{der|en|it|bergamotta}}, from {{der|en|ota|[[بك]] [[آرمود|آرمودی]]|tr=beg armudu|lit=a lord's pear}}, denoting a [[w:de:Bergamotte (Birne)|fattish kind of pear]]. The European word developed the sense of a fruit of a certain citrus cultivar in the late 17th century.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# A tree of the orange family ({{taxlink|Citrus × limon|nothospecies}}, syn. {{taxlink|Citrus bergamia|species}}), having a roundish or pear-shaped fruit, from the rind of which an essential oil of delicious odor is extracted, much prized as a perfume.
# The fruit from the bergamot tree
# The essence or perfume made from the fruit.
# A variety of snuff perfumed with bergamot.
#* {{RQ:Cowper Task
|passage=The better hand {{...}} gives the nose its '''bergamot'''.}}
# Either of two plants of the mint family noted for their bergamot-like scent:
## {{taxlink|Mentha × piperita|nothospecies}}, [[nothosubspecies]] ''citrata'', more commonly known as {{vern|bergamot mint}}
## {{taxfmt|Monarda didyma|species}}, also known as {{vern|American bergamot}} or [[bee balm]].
# A variety of pear.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=a. 1724|title=Philosophical Transactions|passage=One of my Neighbours has a '''Bergamot''' Pear Tree, that was brought from England in a Box , about the Year 1643}}

